import{_ as t,o,c as s,a3 as a}from"./chunks/framework.D1GAkLkK.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Backend protocol","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/backend-protocol.md","filePath":"reference/backend-protocol.md"}'),d={name:"reference/backend-protocol.md"};function i(c,e,n,h,r,l){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(`<h1 id="backend-protocol" tabindex="-1">Backend protocol <a class="header-anchor" href="#backend-protocol" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Backend protocol&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A plugin backend keeps the state of OpsTruss somewhere other than <code>.opstruss/state/</code>, such as a bucket that a team and CI share. It is a program that <code>backend.run</code> of its <code>opstruss.plugin.yaml</code> names, and a project uses it through <code>backend:</code>. This page is what such a program can rely on, since 1.1.</p><div class="language-yaml vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">yaml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># opstruss.plugin.yaml</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">opstruss</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;1.1&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">name</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">s3</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">requires</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: [</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">aws</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">jq</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">inputs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">  bucket</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: {</span><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">type</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">string</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">required</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">true</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">backend</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">  run</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: [</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">./s3.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="the-program" tabindex="-1">The program <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-program" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The program&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>OpsTruss starts the program when a command opens the state, once for each backend, and closes its stdin when the command is done with it. It also stops one that sat unused for a minute, and starts it again for the next operation. A program that does not end 10 seconds after its stdin closes gets <code>SIGTERM</code>, then <code>SIGKILL</code>.</li><li>It gets the environment of a <a href="./program-contract#environment">program</a> that runs outside a run: the inputs that <code>with:</code> of <code>backend:</code> gives, as <code>INPUT_&lt;NAME&gt;</code> only, so no file with a credential outlives an OpsTruss that was killed, <code>OPSTRUSS_PLUGIN_DIR</code>, <code>OPSTRUSS_PROJECT</code>, <code>OPSTRUSS_STACK</code>, the stack of the command, and <code>OPSTRUSS_CONTRACT</code>. It gets no facts of a run, since it also serves commands without one, such as <code>opstruss state</code>.</li><li>stdin and stdout carry the protocol; what the program writes to stderr is a warning of the run, or of the command, with the values of secrets hidden. An operation that fails says why in the <code>message</code> of its error.</li><li>An answer that does not come within 5 minutes stops the program, and that operation, as every later one of the command, fails.</li></ul><h2 id="messages" tabindex="-1">Messages <a class="header-anchor" href="#messages" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Messages&quot;">​</a></h2><p>JSON-RPC 2.0, one message a line, as UTF-8. OpsTruss sends one request, waits for its answer, and sends the next; the first is <code>initialize</code>. A message is at most 64 MiB, its newline included: a program that writes a larger one is stopped, and the command fails.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Params</th><th>Result</th><th>Errors, in <code>error.data.code</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>initialize</code></td><td><code>protocol: 1</code></td><td><code>protocol: 1</code>, <code>location</code>, <code>capabilities</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>get</code></td><td><code>key</code></td><td><code>data</code>, <code>etag</code></td><td><code>not_found</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>put</code></td><td><code>key</code>, <code>data</code>, and <code>if_match</code> or <code>if_none_match: true</code></td><td><code>etag</code></td><td><code>conflict</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>delete</code></td><td><code>key</code>, <code>if_match</code></td><td><code>{}</code></td><td><code>conflict</code>, <code>not_found</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>list</code></td><td><code>prefix</code></td><td><code>keys</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>scan</code></td><td><code>prefix</code></td><td><code>items</code>, each with <code>key</code> and <code>data</code></td><td><code>unsupported</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>lock</code></td><td><code>key</code>, <code>holder</code></td><td><code>token</code></td><td><code>locked</code>, with <code>holder</code> and <code>token</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>unlock</code></td><td><code>key</code>, <code>token</code></td><td><code>{}</code></td><td><code>conflict</code>, <code>not_found</code></td></tr></tbody></table><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>→ {&quot;jsonrpc&quot;:&quot;2.0&quot;,&quot;id&quot;:1,&quot;method&quot;:&quot;initialize&quot;,&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;protocol&quot;:1}}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>← {&quot;jsonrpc&quot;:&quot;2.0&quot;,&quot;id&quot;:1,&quot;result&quot;:{&quot;protocol&quot;:1,&quot;location&quot;:&quot;s3://acme-opstruss/platform/&quot;,&quot;capabilities&quot;:{&quot;scan&quot;:true}}}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>→ {&quot;jsonrpc&quot;:&quot;2.0&quot;,&quot;id&quot;:2,&quot;method&quot;:&quot;get&quot;,&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;key&quot;:&quot;prod/_meta&quot;}}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>← {&quot;jsonrpc&quot;:&quot;2.0&quot;,&quot;id&quot;:2,&quot;error&quot;:{&quot;code&quot;:-32000,&quot;message&quot;:&quot;no such key: prod/_meta&quot;,&quot;data&quot;:{&quot;code&quot;:&quot;not_found&quot;}}}</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li><code>location</code> names where the state is, without credentials: it shows in messages, and with the source of the plugin it is what makes two backends the same one. An answer with another <code>protocol</code> stops the command.</li><li><code>capabilities</code> names the methods that a program may leave out and serves: <code>{&quot;scan&quot;: true}</code>, or <code>{}</code>. OpsTruss sends no method that it does not name.</li><li>A key is a path relative to the location, such as <code>prod/_meta</code>, <code>prod/web/state.json</code>, <code>prod/web/state.lock</code>, or <code>prod/_kv/&lt;name&gt;</code>, and its <code>data</code> is JSON text.</li><li><code>put</code> replaces the whole of a key. With <code>if_match</code>, it writes only when the key still has that etag; with <code>if_none_match</code>, only when there is no key; otherwise the error is <code>conflict</code>. <code>delete</code> takes <code>if_match</code> the same way. An etag is opaque to OpsTruss, and changes with each write.</li><li><code>get</code> and <code>list</code> see each write that finished, from any client.</li><li><code>list</code> gives every key that starts with <code>prefix</code>, at any depth, in any order. A lock that is held is a key there, and one that was let go is not: a plan sees which units another run holds in what it listed, without another operation.</li><li><code>scan</code> gives the keys that <code>list</code> gives, each with its <code>data</code>, so a plan reads a stack, and a <code>kv</code> write the names of its stack, in one operation rather than a <code>get</code> for each key. A key that goes while it scans, such as a lock that was let go, may be left out. It is no snapshot of one moment and has no etags: OpsTruss writes nothing from it, and reads a key with <code>get</code> before it writes. A program that cannot give a prefix in one answer, such as one whose answer would be larger than a message can be, answers <code>unsupported</code>: OpsTruss then lists the prefix and gets each key that it reads.</li><li><code>lock</code> does not wait: on a key that is locked, it answers <code>locked</code> with the <code>holder</code> that the lock keeps, the object OpsTruss gave, and its <code>token</code>. A lock stays when its holder dies, so another process, as <code>opstruss unlock</code>, can let it go. <code>unlock</code> lets go only with the token of the lock: <code>conflict</code> for another token, <code>not_found</code> for a lock that is not there. A token is of one lock alone: a lock taken again, also by the same holder, gets another token, so an unlock that read the token of the lock before does not let it go. A program whose token comes from the content of an object, as an ETag of S3, keeps a random value in each lock.</li><li>Any other error fails the operation, with its <code>message</code>. A method that the program does not know is error <code>-32601</code>, so a later version can add methods as capabilities.</li></ul><h2 id="the-probe" tabindex="-1">The probe <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-probe" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The probe&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>opstruss init</code> and <code>opstruss doctor</code> try the contract before a stack starts in a backend, on keys under <code>_probe/&lt;id&gt;/</code> that no stack reads:</p><ol><li><code>put</code> with <code>if_none_match</code> of a key as deep as a record, <code>_probe/&lt;id&gt;/unit/state.json</code>, then <code>get</code> gives what it wrote, with its etag, <code>list</code> of the prefix gives the key, and <code>scan</code> gives it with what was written; a second <code>put</code> with <code>if_none_match</code> is a <code>conflict</code>.</li><li><code>put</code> with the etag writes, and <code>get</code> gives what it wrote, with its new etag; <code>put</code> and <code>delete</code> with the old etag are a <code>conflict</code>.</li><li><code>delete</code> with the etag, then <code>get</code> is <code>not_found</code>.</li><li>A second <code>lock</code> is <code>locked</code>, with the holder and the token of the first, and <code>list</code> and <code>scan</code> give the key of the lock.</li><li><code>unlock</code> with another token is a <code>conflict</code>; with the token, the lock goes, and the same holder takes it again with another token.</li><li><code>list</code> and <code>scan</code> of the prefix are empty.</li></ol><p>The steps of <code>scan</code> run only for a program that names it in <code>capabilities</code>. A storage that fails a step, such as one that deletes an object whose etag does not match, is refused with the step, before any state is written there.</p>`,14)])])}const u=t(d,[["render",i]]);export{k as __pageData,u as default};
